"""Methods edited for scientific argument and continuity; primary settings unchanged."""

METHODS = '''## Methods

### Cohort and reference diagnosis

We analyzed version 1.0.0 of the Parkinson's Disease Smartwatch dataset (PADS) [5]. The source study enrolled participants at a tertiary center in Münster, Germany, during 2018–2021. The release documentation describes ICD-10-based clinical diagnoses confirmed by board-certified neurologists. Of the 469 released participants, all 276 with PD and 114 with differential diagnoses (DD) entered this analysis. The remaining 79 healthy controls contributed to data-integrity checks but not to prediction-model development or evaluation. We used the supplied clinical labels without readjudication or neuropathological confirmation. The study concerned classification of established diagnoses, rather than prediction of incident disease.

The DD group included other movement disorders (n=60), essential tremor (n=28), atypical parkinsonism (n=15) and multiple sclerosis (n=11). Age, recorded gender and responses to all 30 binary NMSQuest items were available for every participant. These 32 variables formed the common clinical input set. Diagnosis, disease-specific metadata, identifiers and measurements from tasks not yet acquired were excluded from prediction inputs. Table 1 describes the cohort.

### Signal quality and feature representation

All 469 released participants had bilateral recordings for all 11 tasks. Five wrist-task records contained gaps exceeding 0.1 seconds, affecting four participants in the PD/DD analysis. These participants were retained. Feature extraction discarded the first 0.5 seconds, divided recordings at longer gaps and resampled each remaining segment at 100 Hz. Interpolation and filtering did not cross a gap. Segments shorter than 200 resampled points contributed no descriptors, although the initially discarded samples remained part of the acquisition-time accounting.

For each wrist and sensor, centered three-axis signals were filtered using a second-order, 0.5-Hz high-pass Butterworth filter applied forward and backward. Seventeen descriptors summarized amplitude, temporal variation and spectral content. Welch spectra [48] used 200-point Hann windows, 100-point overlap and a 400-point Fourier transform. Acceleration and gyroscope descriptors were calculated separately. Bilateral means and absolute differences yielded 68 features per task; every resulting value was finite. These engineering summaries were not assumed to be validated clinical biomarkers. Supplementary Methods S1 gives the definitions, segment pooling and spectral-resolution details.

The source-data environment recorded a full-archive SHA-256 check and agreement of 12,689 source members with the official checksum manifest. Within the modeling environment, checks reconciled the returned audit and feature-package hashes, participant labels and identifiers, task availability, clinical inputs and timing arrays. The complete raw archive was not independently rehashed there. Supplementary Methods S8 provides provenance and the immutable input hashes.

### Acquisition constraints and resource measurement

The resource measure was nominal signal-recording time, calculated from the release's record lengths at its stated sampling rate. Three tasks required 20.48 seconds and eight required 10.24 seconds, giving a full-protocol duration of 143.36 seconds. Concurrent recordings from both wrists were charged once. An abbreviated examination began with Relaxed and could acquire whole tasks up to a cap of 71.68 seconds. Task order was preserved, so an earlier task could not be revisited after it had been skipped. LiftHold required StretchHold, and Entrainment required TouchNose. These rules admitted 257 abbreviated subsets.

Acquisition decisions used these known costs, without consulting a test participant's future timestamps. Recorded durations were audited separately. Following an outcome-blind timing check, the original sensitivity plan also included a conservative 61.44-second cap: every admissible subset under that cap was shorter than half of each participant's recorded full examination. The resource measure excluded preparation, instructions, transitions, questionnaire completion, computation and subsequent referral. It therefore describes recording burden rather than total clinical workload.

### Prediction models and acquisition policies

Within each fitting sample, clinical predictors were standardized, and each task's 68 features were standardized and reduced to eight whitened principal components [49]. A separate logistic classifier used the clinical variables and task components available at each state. The 257 admissible subsets, clinical-only state and complete examination required 259 classifiers. All used L2 regularization with C=1, inverse-frequency class weights, the liblinear solver, intercept scaling of 100, tolerance of 10⁻⁶ and at most 2,000 iterations. This common model family allowed acquisition policies to be compared without changing their underlying predictive machinery. Model scores were not assumed to represent calibrated clinical probabilities.

Three-fold cross-fitting within each development sample generated held-out scores for every state. Standardization, principal components and classifiers were fitted anew in each training partition. The optimized fixed examination selected the admissible subset with the highest cross-fitted balanced accuracy at a score threshold of 0.5. Ties favored shorter duration, then fewer tasks and then task order. All test participants in an outer fold received that fold's selected subset.

The adaptive policy used observed information to estimate the loss of each reachable terminal subset. At the current state, it identified the 25 nearest development participants from their clinical inputs and acquired task components. Squared distances were averaged within each block, giving the clinical block and each acquired task block equal total weight. The estimated terminal loss was the inverse-class-frequency-weighted mean squared cross-fitted score error among these neighbors. The policy selected the subset with the smallest loss, using deterministic duration, size and task-order tie breaking. Selection of the current state ended the examination. Otherwise, the earliest additional task in the selected subset was acquired, and the calculation was repeated with the new observation.

Complete development recordings were available for learning the consequences of future acquisitions. For a test participant, future signals could not enter the current score, neighbor search or next-task choice. This conditional-loss rule is a particular implementation of adaptive acquisition, rather than an implementation of EDDI [9]. Time constrained feasibility and resolved ties; it was not a continuous penalty in the objective.

Comparators included clinical inputs alone, clinical inputs plus Relaxed, the complete examination and a fixed subset selected by class-balanced squared error. A further strategy followed the optimized fixed subset in its original order but allowed stopping at a reachable prefix using the same conditional-loss rule. It thus retained a common task order while allowing the examination to end at different times. Every motion comparator included the common clinical inputs. Clinical-only and complete-examination results provided context outside the minimum-motion and abbreviated-cap constraints, respectively.

### Nested evaluation and output thresholds

Five participant-level outer folds were fixed before model fitting (seed 20260913). Each contained 312 development and 78 test participants, with all wrists and tasks from an individual kept together (Figure 1). Within each outer development set, a further three-fold procedure trained the entire acquisition system on 208 participants and evaluated it on 104. Each of these systems had its own three-fold state-model cross-fit. The resulting middle-held-out predictions were used to select output thresholds. Nesting was necessary because acquisition development and threshold selection are themselves model-building steps whose apparent performance can be optimistic when evaluated on the same observations [17,26].

For each method, a binary cutoff was selected from 0.05 to 0.95 in increments of 0.05 to maximize balanced accuracy on the pooled middle-held-out predictions. Ties favored the value closest to 0.5 and then the lower value. A rejection margin around that cutoff targeted approximately 20% indeterminate outputs in development data. Scores strictly inside the margin were deferred, with tied distances kept together. The acquisition system was then fitted on all 312 development participants; its output cutoff and margin were applied unchanged to the 78 outer-test participants. Deferral occurred at the terminal acquired state, potentially before the resource cap. Test-set coverage was observed rather than constrained.

### Endpoints and original uncertainty analysis

The primary estimand was adaptive-minus-optimized-fixed balanced accuracy of the underlying binary predictions across all 390 participants. Balanced accuracy averaged PD sensitivity and DD specificity, giving the two reference classes equal importance. Each deferred participant retained their underlying binary prediction for this endpoint. Separately, we reported correct clear, wrong clear and indeterminate outputs within each reference class. Coverage was the proportion receiving a clear PD or DD output.

The original 95% percentile intervals used 5,000 class-stratified participant-bootstrap samples of the pooled outer-held-out predictions (seed 20260913), paired across methods. These conditional intervals held the fitted procedures and thresholds fixed. They were supplemented by fold-specific contrasts and were retained unchanged when further analyses were added. The complete-refitting analysis below addresses an additional source of variation; it does not retroactively alter the original interval calculation.

The original sensitivity plan included complete redevelopment without the 30 NMS predictors, nominal caps from 20.48 to 71.68 seconds and omission of the four gap-affected participants from scoring. The last analysis did not refit a clean-cohort model. Age, recorded-gender and DD-subtype analyses were descriptive. The original analysis specification was recorded and hashed before predictive fitting, but was not externally preregistered. Models were implemented in scikit-learn [18]; versions, fitting counts and executable entry points are reported in the supplement.

### Alternative development partitions and model settings

After the primary results were examined, the complete five-fold procedure was repeated under five additional participant partitions (seeds 20260914–20260918). Every representation, state-model cross-fit, acquisition policy and output threshold was fitted again. The same 390 people were evaluated in each partition. The resulting ranges describe sensitivity to development assignment rather than independent replication or confidence intervals [19]. We also counted participants whose binary decision, three-way output or terminal task set changed across these five evaluations.

A subsequent specification fixed six one-factor alternatives before their fits: four or twelve task components, C=0.1 or 10, and fifteen or forty neighbors. Each repeated the complete nested procedure on the original outer folds. The primary configuration remained eight components, C=1 and twenty-five neighbors; no setting was selected by test performance. Reproduction of the original first fold preceded these fits. These analyses examined parameter dependence within the chosen model family.

### Random acquisition and task-assignment controls

Uniform random next-task acquisition retained the learned conditional-loss stopping rule. When continuation was recommended, a whole next task was sampled uniformly from the forward-feasible tasks, after which stopping was reconsidered using the newly observed state. Terminal-path probabilities were integrated exactly over the 257 admissible states. Performance was averaged only after each terminal score had been converted to a binary or three-way decision. Thus, the control did not receive the predictive benefit of averaging scores across several paths while paying for only one. Its own binary cutoff and weighted rejection margin were fitted from rebuilt middle-held-out random-policy predictions.

Two further controls permuted adaptive terminal task masks among outer-test participants. The first permuted within each fold; the second also matched nominal duration and task count. Both retained the marginal task, duration and count distributions, while matching additionally retained each participant's duration and count. A recipient's own signals supplied the score for an assigned mask, with the original adaptive output thresholds retained. Reference labels did not govern mask assignment. Because the mask pools came from the evaluated cohort, these controls served as retrospective allocation diagnostics. Duration-and-count matching retained individualized stopping and tested only the assignment of task identities.

Expected randomized outcomes were used for the main exploratory contrasts. An additional 1,000 whole-cohort realizations per control and partition described variation due to acquisition randomness. Their percentile ranges were simulation reference ranges, not participant-sampling intervals or randomized-trial p values. Supplementary Methods S4 gives the exact-expectation calculations and the limits imposed by empirical permutation pools.

### Probability calibration and deferral consequences

For each acquisition method and outer fold, an unweighted monotone logistic recalibrator was fitted to the 312 middle-held-out scores. The acquired path and original binary decision were retained. Deferral based on distance to the original binary cutoff was compared with confidence in the assigned label, using either raw or recalibrated scores. Development rejection targets were 0%, 10%, 20%, 30% and 40%, with 20% focal. Recalibration and rejection cutoffs used the same development scores, without an independent calibration sample or a test-set rejection quota.

Probability assessment included unweighted Brier score, log loss, binned reliability and descriptive calibration intercept and slope [13]. For each reference class, loss was defined as (clear errors + lambda × deferred)/class size; its mean and maximum across PD and DD were reported. Values of lambda were 0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75 and 1, with 0.5 focal. They represented hypothetical costs of an unresolved decision, rather than patient-derived utilities or clinical net benefit [20].

Two further rules selected separate confidence cutoffs for predicted PD and predicted DD, minimizing mean-class or worst-class development loss under a rejection-count constraint. Reference labels informed development loss, while the predicted label determined which cutoff applied at evaluation. The distinction between average and group-level performance motivated the worst-class objective [12,21]; no guarantees from the cited training algorithms were assumed. Supplementary Methods S6 specifies quota handling and tie rules. These analyses changed deferral while retaining the acquisition paths and underlying binary decisions.
'''

EVIDENCE_STATUS = '''### Analysis chronology and inferential scope

The additional partitions, model settings, random controls, calibration analyses, deferral allocations and complete participant refits were specified sequentially after earlier results had been examined. Their train–test separation protects within-fold evaluation, but does not make the iterative research process confirmatory. All specified settings and outcomes are reported, without choosing a favorable replacement primary result. Conditional percentile intervals for the earlier extensions retain fitted models, recalibrators, thresholds and, where applicable, random-control pools. The separate 500-replicate analysis repeats development for the four core acquisition strategies; it does not refit the calibration, class-aware allocation or mask-permutation extensions. No multiplicity-adjusted superiority claim or external-validation claim is made.
'''

